  "worked_solution": "Recall  ${cos}^{2}\u2061x+{sin}^{2}\u2061x=1,$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{sin}^{2}\u2061x+7{cos}^{2}\u2061x & =3{sin}^{2}\u2061x+3{cos}^{2}\u2061x+4{cos}^{2}\u2061x \\\\  & =3({sin}^{2}\u2061x+{cos}^{2}\u2061x)+4{cos}^{2}\u2061x \\\\  & =3+4{cos}^{2}\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}\u2061x$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x=2{cos}^{2}\u2061x-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}\u2061x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(3+4{cos}^{2}\u2061x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[3+\\frac{4}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x+1)]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(5+2\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(5x+\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =[5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})]-[5\\cdot 0+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & =[\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{2})]-[0+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(0)] \\\\  & =[\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+1]-[0+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi +4}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
